From 4f50dafab4eea80ad6fdab40318075eba0a95986 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: FiyeroT <me@fiyero.xyz>
Date: Tue, 29 Sep 2026 14:31:40 +0300
Subject: [PATCH 06/66] dns/udp: one socket per query for servers reached
 without a proxy

The UDP transport sent every query through one shared socket. Public
resolvers limit the rate per source socket: Yandex DNS answers about 20
queries per burst and 20 per second per source port and drops the rest,
so a page load with a few dozen new names lost half of its answers.
Measured through sing-box against 77.88.8.8: 20-22 of 40 answered.

Send each query from its own socket, as dnsmasq, unbound, AdGuard dnsproxy
and the Go resolver do: a random source port and query ID per query
(RFC 5452), the reply must match the ID and the question, and the socket
is closed on every return path, including cancellation. At most 256
sockets are open at once (dnsmasq caps forwarded queries at 150).

This applies when the queries leave through a socket of this process: no
detour, or a detour to a direct outbound, whatever interface or mark it
binds to (the resolver of a podkop VPN section). It is decided at the
first query, as outbounds are created after DNS servers; a legacy server
without a detour goes by the type of the default outbound. Through a
proxy the shared session stays: a new UDP session of VLESS, VMess, Trojan
or SOCKS is a new TCP (and TLS) connection to the proxy server. 200
queries through VLESS+TLS: 1 proxy connection with the shared session,
200 with a socket per query.

Result against 77.88.8.8: 40 of 40 and 100 of 100 per burst, 100% at
50 queries per second; under 200 qps for 5 minutes the socket count
returns to the baseline and memory stays as before.
